    "faqSchema": {
      "Which US states have no personal income tax?": "Eight states have no personal wage income tax: Alaska, Florida, Nevada, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington, and Wyoming.",
      "What is the difference between a flat tax state and a graduated bracket state?": "Flat tax states apply a single rate to all income (e.g. PA 3.07%, IL 4.95%). Graduated states apply progressive slabs (e.g. CA up to 13.3%).",
      "How does state income tax work if I work remotely in another state?": "Taxes are usually owed where work is physically done, though convenience-of-employer states like NY may tax out-of-state remote workers.",
      "What are state tax reciprocity agreements?": "Agreements between neighboring states allowing commuters to pay tax only to their home state without filing non-resident returns.",
      "Are state income taxes deductible on my federal tax return?": "Yes, if itemizing on Schedule A, but total State and Local Tax (SALT) deduction is capped at $10,000 per year.",
      "Do cities and counties charge their own separate income taxes?": "Yes, cities like New York City (up to 3.876%) and Philadelphia (3.75%) levy municipal income taxes in addition to state taxes."
    }
